---
import { ELECTRICITY, HISTORY } from '../../data/electricity';
import { HEATING_ROWS } from '../../data/heating';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { cents, dollars, monthLabel, signedPct } from '../../lib/format';
import { BTU, heatPumpCop } from '../../lib/heating';

if (!HISTORY) throw new Error('the winter guide needs the EIA price history');
const history = HISTORY;
// The most recent full winter in the history: December, January and February.
const decIndex = history.periods.findLastIndex(
  (p, i) => p.endsWith('-12') && i + 2 < history.periods.length,
);
const winter = history.periods.slice(decIndex, decIndex + 3);
const winterLabel = `${monthLabel(winter[0]!)} – ${monthLabel(winter[2]!)}`;
const mean = (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / xs.length;
/** Heating months the year's heat is spread over (November to March). */
const MONTHS = 5;
const HSPF2 = 7.8;
const rows = HEATING_ROWS.map((h) => {
  const series = history.byState[h.code]!;
  const year = mean(series.slice(-12));
  const winterPrice = mean(series.slice(decIndex, decIndex + 3));
  const price = h.prices.electric;
  const resistanceKwh = (h.heat * 1e6) / BTU.kwh / MONTHS;
  const heatPumpKwh = resistanceKwh / heatPumpCop(HSPF2);
  return {
    h,
    price,
    winterVsYear: ((winterPrice - year) / year) * 100,
    resistance: (resistanceKwh * price) / 100,
    heatPump: (heatPumpKwh * price) / 100,
    resistanceKwh,
    heatPumpKwh,
    // 1,500 W space heater, 8 hours a day, 30 days.
    heater: (1.5 * 8 * 30 * price) / 100,
    // A 10 kW backup strip for one hour.
    strip: (10 * price) / 100,
  };
}).sort((a, b) => a.h.name.localeCompare(b.h.name));
const us = (() => {
  const price = mean(history.us.slice(-12));
  const winterPrice = mean(history.us.slice(decIndex, decIndex + 3));
  return { price, winterVsYear: ((winterPrice - price) / price) * 100 };
})();
const higherInWinter = rows.filter((r) => r.winterVsYear > 1).length;
const lowerInWinter = rows.filter((r) => r.winterVsYear < -1).length;
const median = (xs: number[]) => [...xs].sort((a, b) => a - b)[Math.floor(xs.length / 2)]!;
// States whose RECS figure is their census division's (Hawaii) are left out of heating numbers.
const measured = rows.filter((r) => !r.h.heatFromDivision);
const medResistance = median(measured.map((r) => r.resistance));
const medHeatPump = median(measured.map((r) => r.heatPump));
const ratio = heatPumpCop(HSPF2);
const title = 'Why is my electric bill so high in winter?';
const description =
  'Why winter electric bills jump: electric heat, heat pump backup strips, space heaters, longer billing periods and prices, with what each costs in your state.';
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  crumbs={[['Winter electric bill', '/guides/why-is-my-electric-bill-so-high-in-winter/']]}
  jsonLd={[
    {
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: title,
      description,
      url: 'https://wattsplain.com/guides/why-is-my-electric-bill-so-high-in-winter/',
      dateModified: ELECTRICITY.fetchedAt,
      author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Wattsplain', url: 'https://wattsplain.com/' },
      publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Wattsplain', url: 'https://wattsplain.com/' },
    },
  ]}
>
  <h1>Why is my electric bill so high in winter?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    {`In most homes a winter jump comes from heat: electric heat, a heat pump running its backup strips, or space heaters. Price matters less than people expect: in ${winterLabel}, the average residential price was ${signedPct(us.winterVsYear)} against its 12-month average nationally. The numbers below are for your state.`}
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/tools/why-is-my-electric-bill-high/">Compare two of your bills</a> to see how much of
    the change is price and how much is use, or answer{' '}
    <a href="/tools/electric-bill-diagnosis/">four questions</a> to find the likely cause.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Electric heat uses a lot of kWh</h2>
  <p>
    {`Baseboard heaters, an electric furnace and plug-in heaters turn one kWh into ${BTU.kwh.toLocaleString('en-US')} Btu of heat. Spread over November to March, the heat a typical home in the middle state needs comes to about ${dollars(medResistance, 0)} a month with resistance heat. The same home with a heat pump (HSPF2 ${HSPF2}, about ${ratio.toFixed(1)} units of heat per unit of electricity) pays about ${dollars(medHeatPump, 0)}. If your home heats with electricity, a winter bill several times your summer bill can be normal.`}
  </p>

  <h2>2. A heat pump on backup (aux or emergency) heat</h2>
  <p>
    Most heat pumps have electric resistance strips for very cold days, defrost and quick recovery
    after a big thermostat setback. While they run, the heat pump costs as much per unit of heat as
    baseboard heat. A thermostat showing "aux" or "emergency heat" for hours at a time, or a setting
    left on "emergency heat", can double or triple a bill. Turn the thermostat up in small steps
    (2°F at a time) and ask a technician to check the outdoor unit if aux runs on mild days.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Space heaters add up</h2>
  <p>
    {`A 1,500 W heater on high for 8 hours a day uses 360 kWh a month: about ${dollars(median(rows.map((r) => r.heater)), 0)} in the middle state. Two of them double it.`}{' '}
    <a href="/tools/space-heater-vs-central-heat/">
      Check whether a heater saves money with your central heat
    </a>
    .
  </p>

  <h2>4. Longer billing periods and estimated readings</h2>
  <p>
    A bill for 33 days uses more than one for 28. Holidays and weather delay meter reads, and a bill
    based on an estimate can be followed by a catch-up bill. Compare kWh per day, not the total.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Winter prices</h2>
  <p>
    {`Prices move through the year. Comparing ${winterLabel} with the 12-month average, the price was higher in winter in ${higherInWinter} states and lower in ${lowerInWinter} (the rest within 1%). Where it is lower, winter bills are high because of use, not price.`}
  </p>

  <h2>Winter heating costs by state</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Winter heating costs by state">
    <table>
      <caption>{`Typical home in each state: the heat EIA's RECS 2020 measured for a gas-heated home, spread evenly over November to March. Electric homes are often smaller or in milder places, and the coldest month costs more than this average. Price: ${cents(us.price)} U.S. 12-month average; each state's own 12-month average. Strip: one hour of a 10 kW backup strip. –: RECS has no heating figure for the state itself.`}</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>State</th>
          <th>Price</th>
          <th>Winter vs year</th>
          <th>Resistance heat / month</th>
          <th>Heat pump / month</th>
          <th>Space heater / month</th>
          <th>Strip / hour</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={`/electricity-rates/${r.h.slug}/`}>{r.h.name}</a>
            </td>
            <td>{cents(r.price)}</td>
            <td>{signedPct(r.winterVsYear)}</td>
            <td>{r.h.heatFromDivision ? '–' : dollars(r.resistance, 0)}</td>
            <td>{r.h.heatFromDivision ? '–' : dollars(r.heatPump, 0)}</td>
            <td>{dollars(r.heater, 0)}</td>
            <td>{dollars(r.strip)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What to check first</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>kWh on this bill vs the same month last year (not last month).</li>
    <li>Days in the billing period, and whether the reading was estimated.</li>
    <li>Thermostat: is it set to "emergency heat"? How often does it show "aux"?</li>
    <li>
      Space heaters, heated blankets, an electric water heater (colder incoming water in winter).
    </li>
    <li>The price per kWh on the bill: has the supply or delivery rate changed?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Related: <a href="/tools/heating-cost-calculator/">heat pump vs gas, propane and oil</a> ·{' '}
    <a href="/electricity-rates/price-changes/">price increases by state</a> ·{' '}
    <a href="/methodology/">how we calculate</a>
  </p>
</Base>
